problem link : https://leetcode.com/problems/shortest-path-in-binary-matrix/description/
Approach
We can treat every 0 cell as a node in a graph.

From each cell, we can move to any of its 8 neighboring cells (horizontal, vertical, or diagonal), as long as the neighboring cell is also 0.

Since every valid move has the same cost of 1, this is an unweighted shortest path problem, so we can use BFS.

We store (row, col, dist) in the queue, where dist is the number of cells in the path from (0, 0) to the current cell.

We mark a cell as visited when we add it to the queue so that we never process the same cell multiple times.

When BFS reaches the bottom-right cell, its distance is the shortest path length.

Algorithm
If the starting or destination cell is blocked (1), return -1.

Initialize a queue with (0, 0, 1).

The distance starts at 1 because the starting cell itself counts toward the path length.
Mark (0, 0) as visited.

While the queue is not empty:

Remove the front cell.

If it is the destination, return its distance.

Try all 8 possible directions.

If the new cell is inside the grid, is open (0), and has not been visited:

Mark it visited.
Add it to the queue with distance dist + 1.
If the destination cannot be reached, return -1.

Complexity
Let R be the number of rows and C be the number of columns.

Time: O(R × C)
Each cell is visited at most once, and each cell has at most 8 neighbors.

Space: O(R × C)
For the queue and the visited set.

Code
from collections import deque
class Solution:
    def shortestPathBinaryMatrix(self, grid: List[List[int]]) -> int:
        rows = len(grid)
        cols = len(grid[0])
        directions = [[-1,0], [0,1] , [0,-1], [1,1], [1,-1] ,[1,0],[-1,-1],[-1,1]]
        visited = set()

        if grid[0][0] != 0 or grid[rows-1][cols-1] != 0:
            return - 1
        
        queue = deque()
        queue.append((0,0,1))
        visited.add((0,0))
        while queue:
            row , col , dist  = queue.popleft()
            if row  == rows -1 and col == cols -1:
                return dist
            for dr , dc in directions:
                nr , nc = row + dr , col + dc
                if (nr < 0 or nr >= rows or
                    nc < 0 or nc >= cols or
                    grid[nr][nc] != 0 or 
                    (nr,nc) in visited):
                    continue
                queue.append((nr,nc,dist + 1))
                visited.add((nr,nc))
        return - 1

